  "activity": {
    "id": "act_vectors_projectile",
    "name": "Vectors and Projectile Motion",
    "description": "A guided AR lab for algebra-based General Physics I. Students first explore vectors as abstract objects (magnitude/direction, tip-to-tail addition, and component decomposition), then apply these tools to two-dimensional projectile motion: decomposing launch velocity, observing the independence of horizontal and vertical motion, discovering the 45-degree maximum-range result, completing a targeting challenge, and consolidating intuition in a free sandbox.",
    "workplace": "wp_vectors_projectile",
    "start": "step_vectors_intro",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_vectors_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. What Is a Vector?",
          "description": "A single arrow appears in the workspace with its tail at the origin. Colored ground lines mark the x, y, and z axes. Floating labels show the arrow's magnitude (its length) and its direction (angles relative to each axis). Grab the arrowhead and drag it: notice both values update in real time. A vector has BOTH a size and a direction, unlike a scalar such as temperature or mass. Rotate around the arrow and confirm the vector itself does not change, only your viewpoint. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "axes_ground_lines",
              "poi": "axes_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "single_vector_arrow",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_magnitude",
              "poi": "label_slot",
              "text": "Magnitude: 8.0 units"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_direction",
              "poi": "label_slot",
              "text": "Direction: 55 deg x, 60 deg y, 50 deg z"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_vectors_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "single_vector_arrow"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "label_magnitude"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "label_direction"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "narration_vectors_intro"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_vectors_intro"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_vector_addition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. Adding Vectors Tip-to-Tail",
          "description": "Two vectors appear: a blue vector starting at the origin and a green vector placed tip-to-tail at the head of the blue one. A red resultant arrow runs from the tail of the blue to the head of the green. Grab and reorient either arrow and watch the resultant follow. Then swap the order of the two vectors: the resultant points to the same place, showing that vector addition does not depend on order. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "tip_to_tail_addition",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_magnitude",
              "poi": "label_slot",
              "text": "Resultant magnitude: 11.4 units"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_addition",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "tip_to_tail_addition"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "label_magnitude"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "narration_addition"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_vector_addition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_decomposition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Decomposition: Addition in Reverse",
          "description": "A single vector appears in a neutral color. Three color-coded component arrows emerge from the origin along the x, y, and z axes, laid tip-to-tail so that x starts at the origin, y at the tip of x, and z at the tip of y, ending at the head of the original vector. Resize each component along its axis and watch the original update. Decomposition is just addition reversed: the components add back to the original. In two dimensions, the components and the magnitude form a right triangle. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "component_decomposition",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_magnitude",
              "poi": "label_slot",
              "text": "Magnitude = sqrt(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_decomposition",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "vector_workspace",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_decomposition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_projectile_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. From Vectors to Projectiles",
          "description": "The 3D workspace gives way to a 2D vertical plane with labeled horizontal and vertical axes. Because gravity acts only downward and no horizontal force acts, the entire motion stays in this plane, so a projectile problem is genuinely two-dimensional. A ball rests on the ground line at the left, with an initial velocity vector arrow pointing up and to the right at a launch angle. Point your device at the ball marker to place the scene, then click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vertical_plane_grid",
              "poi": "plane_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_velocity_vector",
              "poi": "velocity_origin"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_projectile_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "augmentation": "projectile_velocity_vector"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "augmentation": "narration_projectile_intro"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "projectile_ball"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_projectile_intro"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_flight_demo",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Independence of Horizontal & Vertical Motion",
          "description": "The launch velocity is decomposed into a horizontal component (right) and a vertical component (up). Launch the ball and watch the live component arrows: the horizontal arrow keeps a constant length while the vertical arrow shrinks during ascent, reaches zero at the peak, then grows downward during descent. A faint parabolic trail is drawn. Switch to an overhead view to see the parabola collapse into a straight line. Horizontal and vertical motions are independent. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_independence",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "augmentation": "narration_independence"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_flight_demo"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_max_range",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Discovering the 45-Degree Maximum Range",
          "description": "Use the control panel to set the launch angle (0 to 90 degrees) and the launch speed, then press launch. The trajectory is drawn and the range is displayed, with a history panel recording each attempt. Try several angles at a fixed speed. Notice that angles symmetric about 45 degrees (30 and 60, 20 and 70) give the same range, and that 45 degrees produces the maximum range because the horizontal and vertical velocity components are equal. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "range_control_panel",
              "poi": "control_slot"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_max_range",
              "poi": "plane_center",
              "text": "Max range at 45 deg"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "augmentation": "range_control_panel"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo"
            },
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "augmentation": "label_max_range"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_max_range"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_targeting",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "7. Targeting Challenge",
          "description": "A target appears on the ground plane a set distance away at the same elevation. Set the launch angle and speed to hit it. For any target distance there are usually two solutions - a complementary pair such as 30 and 60 degrees - so try to find both for at least one target if you like, though a single hit advances you. Point your device at the target anchor to register it, then hit the target to advance."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "landing_target",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "target_marker_flag",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "range_control_panel",
              "poi": "control_slot"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "landing_target",
              "augmentation": "target_marker_flag"
            },
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "augmentation": "range_control_panel"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "landing_target"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_targeting"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_sandbox",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "8. Free-Exploration Sandbox",
          "description": "All constraints are removed. Control every parameter: launch angle, launch speed, initial height (ground up to about 50 meters), and gravity strength (zero to five times Earth's gravity). Launch freely and watch the trajectories with live component arrows. Investigate: what happens to the range when gravity is halved? What does the path look like under very strong gravity? Launched from a 50-meter platform, does 45 degrees still maximize range, or does the optimal angle shift? Click Next to review the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "sandbox_control_panel",
              "poi": "control_slot"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "launch_pad",
              "augmentation": "sandbox_control_panel"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "augmentation": "projectile_flight_demo"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_sandbox"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "9. Lab Recap",
          "description": "A summary board reviews the six concept areas covered: (1) vectors as quantities with magnitude and direction, (2) vector addition by the tip-to-tail method, (3) vector decomposition as reversed addition along chosen axes, (4) applying decomposition to projectile motion, (5) the independence of horizontal and vertical motion under gravity, and (6) the 45-degree maximum-range result. Each area is highlighted in turn as it is reviewed. Click Finish to end the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
